    <string name="advice_note">Trying: %1$s. %2$s</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_kicks_back">Fork kicks back after landings and big hits</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_kicks_back_why">The fork returns too fast after deep compression. More rebound damping calms it down.</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_packs_down">Fork sinks into its travel on repeated hits</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_packs_down_why">Rebound is too slow: the fork cannot recover before the next hit. Less rebound damping lets it come back faster.</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_harsh">Harsh on roots and rocks, hands get tired</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_harsh_why">Fast small hits meet too much compression damping. Opening the high-speed compression lets the fork absorb them.</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_bottoms_out">Fork bottoms out on big hits</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_bottoms_out_why">The end of travel is not progressive enough. A volume token makes it firmer without changing sag; more compression damping also helps.</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_unused_travel">Fork never uses its full travel</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_unused_travel_why">The end of travel is too progressive or compression is too firm. Removing a token or opening compression frees up the travel.</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_dives">Fork dives under braking and on steep sections</string>
    <string name="symptom_fork_dives_why">Slow weight shifts push the fork deep. More low-speed compression damping keeps it higher in its travel.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_kicks_back">Rear kicks up after jumps and drops</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_kicks_back_why">The shock returns too fast after deep compression. More rebound damping keeps the rear wheel planted.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_packs_down">Rear sinks into its travel on repeated hits</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_packs_down_why">Rebound is too slow for repeated hits. Less rebound damping lets the shock recover between them.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_harsh">Rear feels harsh on small bumps</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_harsh_why">Compression damping is too firm for fast small hits. Open the high-speed compression.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_bottoms_out">Shock bottoms out hard</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_bottoms_out_why">Not enough support at the end of travel. A volume token (air shocks) or more compression damping adds it.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_unused_travel">Shock never uses its full travel</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_unused_travel_why">The end of travel is too progressive or compression is too firm. Removing a token or opening compression frees up the travel.</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_bobs">Rear bobs while pedalling</string>
    <string name="symptom_shock_bobs_why">Pedalling forces are slow movements. More low-speed compression damping calms them; a climb switch, if you have one, does the same on climbs.</string>
